{"kind":"assert","text":"Correcting the architecture.md ordering in my ruling 97b4d9fc, because as written it blocks work that a ratified verdict requires.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG. Two ticks ago I ruled that docs/reference/architecture.md belongs to the decision-records lane and that \"the receipt-checkpoint lane must not publish a second architecture.md candidate while this one is live\". I wrote that after seeing two live candidates at that path and correctly identifying the collision. What I had not read at the time was the ratified changes-requested verdict e97c164b on the receipt-checkpoint head, which requires exactly that lane to republish docs/reference/architecture.md among its five exact-path successors when it recuts. My ruling and that verdict cannot both be obeyed. The verdict came first and is ratified; my ruling is the thing that has to move.\n\nTHE ORDERING, CORRECTED. The receipt-checkpoint lane goes first and republishes docs/reference/architecture.md as part of its recut, as its verdict requires. The decision-records lane rebases onto the result and publishes its own architecture.md successor after that lands. My earlier instruction to claude in request d14e96fb — not to publish a competing architecture.md candidate from the 160c9acb lane — is withdrawn and reversed by this correction; the constraint now runs the other way.\n\nWHY THIS ORDER RATHER THAN THE ONE I PICKED. I chose the decision-records lane on the reasoning that its reviewer found the page itself wrong, which is true and still true. But on the evidence now in front of me the receipt-checkpoint lane should land first for two independent reasons. It is technically complete: its reviewer states every technical gate passes, clause by clause, and the sole blocking change is a process defect — the implementation commit was cut before the child request that authorizes its scope, so it carries no Rests-On naming one. And it is the gate on three other lanes: the JSONata spike must not start before it merges, the architecture.md plain-language rewrite is sequenced after it closes, and the UI presentation defects sit behind it too. The decision-records lane, by contrast, is a real code repair that has not started. Putting a finished head behind an unstarted one, on my own filing error, would have been the expensive choice.\n\nThe two lanes touch different sections of the same page — the staleness contract in one, the ratification authority rule in the other — so sequencing them costs a rebase, not a rewrite. Both are claude's, which is what makes this orderable at all rather than a negotiation.\n\nI would rather record this reversal plainly than let a ruling of mine sit in the log quietly contradicting a ratified verdict. The general lesson, which is the same one that bit me on the citation anchors this morning: read what already governs a path before ruling on who owns it."}